$DIR/main.p:18:9: cannot return m as an interface: an ordinary interface only borrows the map, whose reference lend drops when it returns
    m (declared at main.p:17) is a map[string]int, a counted reference to a
    table: an ordinary interface that lends the map does not count a reference
    of its own, so it may not outlive m, whose reference is dropped at the end
    of its scope in lend. Converting m to an ordinary interface lends it: the
    interface points at the map's table without a reference of its own.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Return the map itself, changing the result type to map[string]int: the
    caller gets a reference of its own:
        return m
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 6.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3001" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:24:2: cannot store &n in local["n"], which outlives n
    &n is a pointer to n. n is a local variable of main (declared at main.p:22).
    When main returns, n's memory is reclaimed, but a map keeps its keys and
    elements in a table on the heap, which every copy of the map shares and
    which may live longer, so it would be left pointing at reclaimed memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store the value itself instead of a view of n, changing the map's element
    type to match.
    Or keep what the map refers to in memory that lives as long as the map, such
    as a package variable, or in a counted reference (shared[T] or synced[T])
    stored in the map.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 6.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:25:2: cannot pass &n to remember, which keeps it after returning
    &n is a pointer to n. n is a local variable of main (declared at main.p:22).
    remember stores p in m["last"] (main.p:14), so &n would outlive n.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If remember should keep its own copy of n, change it to take the value, not
    a pointer, or an owner it can keep: an owned[int] from new, a shared[int],
    or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 6.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:28:30: cannot store {...} in an element of the map literal, which outlives buf
    {...} points into buf. buf is a local array of main (declared at main.p:27).
    When main returns, buf's memory is reclaimed, but a map keeps its keys and
    elements in a table on the heap, which every copy of the map shares and
    which may live longer, so it would be left pointing at reclaimed memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store the value itself instead of a view of buf, changing the map's element
    type to match.
    Or keep what the map refers to in memory that lives as long as the map, such
    as a package variable, or in a counted reference (shared[T] or synced[T])
    stored in the map.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 6.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)
